# Every text file is stored with LF. An editor on Windows that saves CRLF
# would otherwise commit a diff that changes every line. This rule comes first,
# so the rules after it can mark a file type as text without the detection.
* text=auto eol=lf

# The rules below name the file types where a CRLF checkout is a broken file
# and not a cosmetic difference, and they say why.
#
# A shell script checked out with CRLF fails at the first line with
# `$'\r': command not found`, and the container gates in scripts/ run on a Linux
# CI runner from a working copy that may have been created on Windows.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Same reasoning: RUN lines are passed to /bin/sh inside the build container.
Dockerfile text eol=lf
Dockerfile.* text eol=lf

# Go tooling writes LF and checks for it. A CRLF checkout makes gofmt and
# goimports report every file as unformatted, so `make lint` fails on a fresh
# Windows clone while CI, which runs on Linux, sees nothing. The same applies
# to the committed OpenAPI document and the generated error-code artefacts,
# which are compared byte for byte against what the generator emits.
*.go text eol=lf
go.mod text eol=lf
go.sum text eol=lf
*.json text eol=lf
*.md text eol=lf
*.mdx text eol=lf
*.yml text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf

# Baselines are compared byte for byte by the arch gates, so autocrlf must not touch them.
scripts/*.baseline text eol=lf

# The client package's generated files are compared byte for byte by
# `cmd/generate-client-sdk -check`, the same way as the artefacts above. The
# same generator writes the example clients under examples/, so those files
# need the same line endings.
packages/typescript/**/*.ts text eol=lf
examples/*/client/**/*.ts text eol=lf

# `go run ./cmd/generate-schema` in packages/graphql writes this file
# with LF on every run, so a CRLF checkout leaves the tree modified after the
# generator has written the same schema back.
packages/graphql/schema.graphql text eol=lf

# The component library's build rewrites this file with LF on every run. With a
# CRLF checkout, each build leaves the tree modified and the binary's version
# reads `+dirty`.
packages/svelte/src/i18n/catalogues.ts text eol=lf

# A copy of another project's files, held to their SHA-256 in SHA256SUMS
# (extensions/server-slicing/ui/vendor/remus-wasm-io/README.md). Git converts
# no line ending in them, so a checkout holds the bytes the sums describe.
extensions/server-slicing/ui/vendor/remus-wasm-io/** -text
